          "provider_id": "4b3525d4-3f5c-4459-9284-f5647c6dd6d9_b90fc2a63080407088db4d8be4dee12e",
          "status": "completed_http200_stop",
          "full_final": "To solve this problem, we need to determine which face of the cube is opposite the face labeled **X**.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the layout of the net**\nThe net consists of a central horizontal row of three squares labeled **U**, **V**, and **W**.\n- **Y** is attached to the top of **W**.\n- **X** is attached to the bottom of **V**.\n- **Z** is attached to the bottom of **X**.\n\n**Step 2: Use the \"skip-one\" rule for cube nets**\nIn any straight line of squares in a cube net, alternate squares form opposite faces.\n- In the horizontal row (U - V - W), **U** and **W** are separated by V, so **U is opposite W**.\n- In the vertical column starting from the top (Y - W - ...), there isn't a direct 3-square strip. However, let's trace the vertical strip passing through X and Z: **V - X - Z**.\n- In this vertical strip, **V** and **Z** are separated by X. Therefore, **V is opposite Z**.\n\n**Step 3: Determine the opposite of X**\nA cube has 6 faces forming 3 opposite pairs. We have identified two pairs so far:\n1.  **U** $\\leftrightarrow$ **W**\n2.  **V** $\\leftrightarrow$ **Z**\n\nThe remaining two faces are **X** and **Y**. Therefore, they must form the third opposite pair.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe face opposite the face labeled **X** is **Y**.\n\nComparing this result to the given choices:\nA: Z\nB: U\nC: V\nD: W\nE: Y\n\nThe correct option is **E**.\n\nE:Y"
